#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128790 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/id #uuid "64c86335-cc36-5b99-a8e3-f2658435b29b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12401" :question/problem "The triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/d9125842cf8cd48196ce9ab2ae311a32.png)\n\n$\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\int_0^1\\int_{\\sqrt{z}}^1\\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x$, then $z$, and then $y$." :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: \\sqrt{z} \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq y^2-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\int_a^b\\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)}\\int_{u_1(y,z)}^{u_2(y,z)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x$, the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\int_a^b\\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)}\\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/1de21d0b8286065d87483c152533f036.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: \\sqrt{z} \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z$ to $\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y$, we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y)$, is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{z} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad z = y^2\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z\\leq y^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/a9c473cff1bba6666cf90eca138e4e13.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\int_0^1 \\int_{0}^{y^2} \\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128792 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/id #uuid "f8b32b74-b82a-5c07-b244-945189e2bcc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12403" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $z$, then $y$, and then $x$, express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\int_{y^2}^{1} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y + \\int_0^1 \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n$\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x$.\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration\n\n$$\nR = R_1\\cup R_2\n$$\n\nwhere $R_1$ and $R_2$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{1} &= {(x, y, z): - 1 \\le y \\le 0, y^{2} \\le x \\le 1, x - 1 \\le z \\le y^{2}}, \\\\\nR_{2} &= {(x, y, z): 0 \\le y \\le 1, y \\le x \\le 1, x - 1 \\le z \\le y^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z$, the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/8a2a53019675f847983cf091720b77e6.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type II regions in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 0, \\quad y^2 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\} \\:\\cup\\: \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad y \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x$, we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the lower boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$, is\n\n$$\nx=y^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y = -\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nand the upper boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=y \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y =x\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad -\\sqrt{x} \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/2a5803a3e93d07a1a44e35de17918573.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1}} \\int_{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{-\\sqrt{x}}}^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x}} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128793 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/id #uuid "72795f5f-0d15-52e1-bfd8-997ae8e20265" :question/math-academy-id "e-12404" :question/problem "Evaluate\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y$.\n\n*Hint: Change change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x$.*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n$\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} 3e^{x^3} \\:\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x$.\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: y \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq y \\right\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z$, the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n$\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\:\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/bb0a1978ac6000ee6b166d46b4d5a7b0.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad y \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x$, we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $y = y(x)$, is\n\n$$\nx = y \\qquad\\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n$y = 0$.\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/9e35eedb89fe4cd2e1aca34b540f9d85.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{y}^{1}∫_{0}^{y}3e^{x^{3}}dzdxdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}∫_{0}^{y}3e^{x^{3}}dzdydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}3e^{x^{3}}[∫_{0}^{y}dz]dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}3e^{x^{3}}[z]_{0}^{y}dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}3ye^{x^{3}}dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}3e^{x^{3}}[∫_{0}^{x}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}3e^{x^{3}}[\\frac{1}{2}y^{2}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}x^{2}e^{x^{3}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}[e^{x^{3}}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac67bf19-0354-51fd-b6ba-285382f59111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x^{2}+y}}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126585} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1eb3ad-69a6-549c-b927-6b926ee6e1a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126586} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9f8c9f-f08e-5430-a8a7-80876465704c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126587} {:answer/id #uuid "29a769e1-3aa3-5db1-9000-5f9d5ffe61ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{y^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126588} {:answer/id #uuid "412f00e2-be13-5c92-8b02-4cbe54888137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x^{2}+y}}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc9f8c9f-f08e-5430-a8a7-80876465704c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126587} :answer-field/id #uuid "683adc15-d582-59cc-a8c2-3bc724db908e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126584}] :question/id #uuid "318d728a-5f9a-5d33-846c-76ae520cec78" :question/math-academy-id "q-162226" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/q-162226.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x, y, z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x^{2}}∫_{0}^{x^{2} + y}f(x, y, z)dzdydx$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z$, then $x$, and then $y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8335bf15-b7ec-5325-b78e-8b855d45c996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126591} {:answer/id #uuid "a0303244-9248-5829-b1aa-b977399244bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126592} {:answer/id #uuid "efdb629a-bfb1-5457-bb56-973b2bbdb505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126593} {:answer/id #uuid "18cde4fd-b4ae-510c-951a-1b51325c7b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126594} {:answer/id #uuid "44d12730-261e-5f3c-a3e4-4026931916f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efdb629a-bfb1-5457-bb56-973b2bbdb505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126593} :answer-field/id #uuid "365cbf84-2149-55dd-8287-bd41d84dd05e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126590}] :question/id #uuid "c00cd44a-d34c-5f6e-aacf-b4ef475837d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162253" :question/problem "Evaluate\n$∫_{0}^{1}∫_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}∫_{0}^{e^{z/x}}dydxdz$.\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $dydzdx$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c333469a-b29c-5ef3-a90c-17540bf0671b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{1}^{1 + y}∫_{1 + y - z}^{2}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126597} {:answer/id #uuid "1b77656b-1e7c-55e6-a9e2-4865ce77ca77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{y 3}∫_{1 + y - z 3}^{2}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126598} {:answer/id #uuid "209229a8-5739-571b-864a-63916f92c779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{y 3}∫_{1 + y - z}^{2}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126599} {:answer/id #uuid "6a706add-38bd-5b9a-8860-0cc40089b5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{y 3}^{1}∫_{1 + y - z}^{2}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126600} {:answer/id #uuid "787a8828-a4c1-52e4-8183-5b8e1d7fc59d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{y 3}^{1}∫_{1 + y - z 3}^{2}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a706add-38bd-5b9a-8860-0cc40089b5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{y 3}^{1}∫_{1 + y - z}^{2}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126600} :answer-field/id #uuid "69ce1705-2585-52a3-b09d-66c84f2db514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126596}] :question/id #uuid "b08713a3-4d0e-55df-a71c-6cc156f3ac58" :question/math-academy-id "q-162527" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/q-162527.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x, y, z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}∫_{1 + y - z}^{2}f(x, y, z)dxdydz$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x$, then $z$, and then $y$."}]}
